nce</article-title><source>Child Health Behavior: A Behavioral Pediatrics Perspective</source><year>1986</year><publisher-name>John Wiley, New York, NY</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B13"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bouchard</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Shepherd</surname><given-names>RJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Stephens</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Sutton</surname><given-names>JR</given-names></name><name><surname>Mcpherson</surname><given-names>BD</given-names></name></person-group><source>Exercise, Fitness and Health: A Consensus of Current Knowledge</source><year>1990</year><publisher-name>Human Kinetics Books, Champaigne, Ill</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B14"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Baranowski</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Cullen</surname><given-names>KW</given-names></name><name><surname>Nicklas</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Thompson</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Baranowski</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>School-based obesity prevention: A blueprint for taming the epidemic</article-title><source>Am J Health Behav</source><year>2002</year><volume>26</volume><fpage>486</fpage><lpage>493</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12437023</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B15"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hagger</surname><given-names>MS</given-names></name><name><surname>Chatzisarantis</surname><given-names>NLD</given-names></name><name><surname>Biddle</surname><given-names>SJH</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A meta-analytic review of the theories of reasoned action and planned behavior in physical activity: predictive validity and contribution of additional variables</article-title><source>Journal of Sport and Exercise Psychology</source><year>2002</year><volume>24</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>12</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hausenblas</surname><given-names>HA</given-names></name><name><surname>Carron</surname><given-names>AV</given-names></name><name><surname>Mack</surname><given-names>DE</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Application of the theories of reasoned action and planned behavior to exercise behavior: A meta analysis</article-title><source>Journal of Sport and Exercise Psychology</source><year>1997</year><volume>19</volume><fpage>36</fpage><lpage>51</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ajzen</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Fishbein</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><source>Understanding Attitudes and Predicting Social Behavior</source><year>1980</year><publisher-name>Englewood Cliffs (NJ): Prentice-Hall</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ajzen</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The Theory of Planned Behavior</article-title><source>Organizational Behavior and Human Decision Processes</source><year>1991</year><volume>50</volume><fpage>179</fpage><lpage>211</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/0749-5978(91)90020-T</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Culos-Reed</surname><given-names>SN</given-names></name><name><surname>Gyurcsik</surname><given-names>NC</given-names></name><name><surname>Brawley</surname><given-names>LR</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Singer RN, Hausenblas HA, Janelle CM</surname></name></person-group><article-title>Using theories of motivated behavior to understand physical activity</article-title><source>Handbook of Sport Psychology</source><edition>2</edition><publisher-name>New York: John Wiley &#x00026; Sons</publisher-name><fpage>695</fpage><lpage>717</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Downs</surname><given-names>DS</given-names></name><name><surname>Hausenblas</surname><given-names>HA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Elicitation studies and the theory of planned behavior: A systematic review of exercise beliefs</article-title><source>Psychology of Sport and Exercise</source><year>2005</year><volume>6</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>31</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.psychsport.2003.08.001</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Darker</surname><given-names>CD</given-names></name><name><surname>French</surname><given-names>DP</given-names></name><name><surname>Longdon</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Morris</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Eves</surname><given-names>FF</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Are beliefs elicited biased by question order? A theory of planning behavior belief elicitation study about walking in the UK general population</article-title><source>British Journal of Health Psychology</source><year>2007</year><volume>12</volume><fpage>93</fpage><lpage>110</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17288668</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1348/135910706X100458</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sutton</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>French</surname><given-names>DP</given-names></name><name><surname>Hennings</surname><given-names>SJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Mitchell</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Wareham</surname><given-names>NJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Griffin</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Hardeman</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>Kinmonth</surname><given-names>AL</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Eliciting salient beliefs in research on the theory of planned behavior: the effect of question world</article-title><source>Current Psychology: Developmental, Learning, Personality, Social</source><year>2003</year><volume>22</volume><fpage>234</fpage><lpage>251</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Steadman</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Rutter</surname><given-names>DR</given-names></name><name><surname>Field</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Individually elicited versus modal normative beliefs in predicting attendance at breast screening: examining the role of belief salience in the Theory of Planned Behavior</article-title><source>British Journal of Health Psychology</source><year>2002</year><volume>7</volume><fpage>317</fpage><lpage>330</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12614503</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1348/135910702760213706</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Middlestadt</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Bhattacharyya</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Rosenbaum</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Fishbein</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Shepherd</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The use of theory based semi-structured elicitation questionnaires: forma